xnu 0224: the CP proves it consumed the bring-up before the ring is published

The same kernel brings the CP up two ways. Measured: in the boot that works
CP_RB_RPTR reaches 12 after the secure-mode switch and the 2000-submission
stress runs; in the boot that does not it stays at 11, the SQE halts on the
switch's own header (`cp sqe stat 0x649 0x70e60001`, ucode pc 0x224, its halt
loop) and no stress line appears at all. The ring was published anyway --
`mondrian_uc_up = 1` sat after the step's `if (rptr >= at)`, not inside it --
so MondrianGPU appeared, every later submission waited out its 300 ms, Mesa's
fd_pipe_new2 failed and the UI silently ran on softpipe: 26 fps, every frame
copied out. A boot with a dead CP has to say so instead.

The read pointer of every step is now read through one bounded helper
(`mondrian_cp_wait_rptr`, 2000 x 1 ms, the window the steps already had inline),
and the ring is published only once the CP has consumed the last bring-up
packet. A failure after the switch is retried once: the SQE is gated, a CP_NOP
goes into the dword it stopped on, the switch is re-issued behind it, and the
SQE is started again through the same raw CP_SQE_CNTL store the start uses,
after the same delay(20). It reuses the read pointer the SQE kept, writes no
GPU, GMU, GDSC or SMMU register, and is not a GPU reset: the CP register port
has just answered the CP_RB_RPTR reads that failed the check. `cpnoretry=1`
reports instead of retrying. If it still fails the ring stays unpublished and
`mondrian_gpu_uc_ready()` stays 0, so Mesa fails at pipe creation instead of
300 ms into the first frame.

The measured reason the switch parks is `CP_APRIV_CNTL`. The a730 SQE
microcode mirrors that register into its per-pipe privileged-opcode mask, and
every boot so far left it at the bootloader's zero, so the CP refused each
privileged packet with `CP_INTERRUPT_STATUS` bit 7 (CP_ILLEGAL_INSTR_ERROR) and
latched nothing in `CP_HW_FAULT` -- one fault, not two. That is the shape the
log has always had: `CP_MEM_WRITE` lands, `CP_EVENT_WRITE` (CACHE_CLEAN) is
refused (`cp int 0x80`), and `setsecure` parks the SQE on ucode pc 0x649, its
switch handler. The ZAP resets the CP block, so the values go in at both of the
vendor's points: with the first publication before the ZAP, and again in the
re-arm before anything is published a second time -- `CP_APRIV_CNTL` 0x4f,
`CP_BV_APRIV_CNTL` 0x0f, `CP_LPAC_APRIV_CNTL` 0x0f, and the LPAC protect
control at 0xb09 (0xb, the protect default the block-0 control at 0x84f already
carries; kgsl keeps that one in `gen7_reg.h` only, so the offset is defined
next to the include). It prints once, in the re-arm, with its read-backs:
`cp apriv 0x4f bv 0xf lpac 0xf lpacprot 0xb`.

Because the re-arm zeroes `CP_RB_WPTR`, the same mode carries cporder's ME_INIT
republish: the ten dwords go back in, the ring is published at 10, the CP is
waited on, and only then is the switch written and published at 10..11. The
protected-mode bracket is dropped from the submission frame and from the
bring-up in this mode: measured with the masks programmed, a `cpfence=mem`
fence and the switch skipped, the bring-up completes (`cp ready`, `cp int 0x0`)
and the 2000-submission stress then freezes the machine with no `uc` line at
all. `CP_SET_PROTECTED_MODE` is the only packet a frame adds over the bring-up
pair, and it is privileged exactly like the CACHE_CLEAN event write: kgsl runs
every submission with protected mode off ("All submissions are run with
protected mode off due to APRIV") and mainline emits none on a7xx. The first
submission prints `uc frame no protected-mode packet`. `cpapriv=0` restores the
previous programming dword for dword: 0x6f/0xf/0xf, no ME_INIT republish unless
`cporder=1`, and the full bracket in the bring-up and in the frame.

Two experiments, independent of each other and off by default, attack the two
candidates left for the difference between the two kinds of boot (everything
before the switch is byte-identical, and `CP_RB_WPTR = 10` is published with the
switch written and published immediately behind it, without ever checking that
the CP retired ME_INIT):

	cporder=1	write and publish the switch only after the CP is seen
			to retire ME_INIT. The ZAP resets the CP and the
			re-arm below zeroes CP_RB_WPTR, so ME_INIT has to be
			offered again before it can be waited for: the same
			ten dwords the first publish wrote are rewritten,
			cleaned and published at 10 first, then waited on.
			Prints `cp meinit rptr N of 10`, or `cp meinit stuck
			rptr N of 10` (measured without the re-publish: rptr 0,
			SQE parked at ucode pc 0x10e on the ME_INIT header);
			on the stuck path nothing else is published, the
			memory packet is skipped, the ring is not published
			and the ready check reports the CP dead.
	gpureset=1	after the ZAP and before the CP registers are re-armed,
			cycle the core through the GMU's own HFI power vote
			(command 30, the message the bring-up prints as `hfi
			power gpu 2 bus 2`): vote 0, a bounded 200 x 1 ms wait,
			vote 1, the same wait. Prints `gpu reset hfi off ack N`
			and `gpu reset hfi on ack N` (1 acknowledged, 0 silent
			for 200 ms, -1 another answer). A core the GMU does not
			grant back stops the bring-up before any CP register
			write.

	cpnosecmode=1	write and publish nothing for the switch at all. The ZAP
			has already put the CP in nonsecure mode by packet, so
			this asks whether the explicit switch is needed on top
			of it: `at` stays 10, the memory packet below is the
			first packet the SQE fetches, and the ready check judges
			the CP by that packet. Prints
			`cp secmode skipped (cpnosecmode)`.

With the switch skipped, the next two packets say where the CP stops: the
memory write retires and its canary lands, and the event write behind it does
not (`cp NOT ready rptr 19 of 23, sqe 0x450 0x70460004`, `fault 0x0 int 0x80
rbbm3 0x0`). Two more gates take that apart, one at a time, both off by
default, and both emit nothing when off so the ring stays byte-identical:

	cpprot=1	put the bring-up's memory packet and its IB probe inside
			the CP_SET_PROTECTED_MODE(0) ... (1) bracket a
			submission carries under `cpapriv=0`
			(`mondrian_cp_prot_enter` / `_exit`, 2 dwords in front
			of each group and 2 behind): a privileged packet issued
			while the CP still believes the context is protected is
			a candidate for a dispatch halt. Prints
			`cp protected-mode bracket on`.
	cpfence=mem	write the fence with a second CP_MEM_WRITE of the same
			32-bit sequence to the same dword instead of
			CP_EVENT_WRITE -- one dword shorter, in the bring-up
			pair, the IB probe, the 2000-submission stress IB and
			the submit path's ring fence; the CPU already polls that
			dword, so nothing else changes. One copy stays an event
			write: the post-init probe (`cp init canary submit`),
			which runs after the stress and is a diagnostic, so it
			keeps asking the CP for CACHE_CLEAN and its answer stays
			comparable. Prints `cp fence by mem write`. What is lost
			is that cache clean: the GPU's own writes are no longer
			flushed before the CPU reads them, so pixels can arrive
			stale while the fence itself lands -- a frame printed
			`1 picture copied out` with a zero picture, or a black
			copy on the panel.
	cpevent=4	write the fence's event dword as CACHE_FLUSH_TS (4,
			payload 0x08000004) instead of CACHE_CLEAN (0x31,
			payload 0x31 | BIT(27)): same packet, same count,
			same 64-bit destination, same value dword. Mainline's
			a6xx/a7xx ring fence (a6xx_gpu.c) and Mesa's a7xx fence
			(freedreno_pipe.c) both use WRITE_ENABLED |
			EV_WRITE_USER_32B | EV_DST_RAM with event 4, and it is
			CACHE_CLEAN the CP refuses with the memory write in
			front of it retired (`cp int 0x80`), so this separates
			"the event write is privileged" from "this event code
			is". The post-init probe keeps CACHE_CLEAN as the
			same-boot control it already is under `cpfence=mem`.
			Prints `cp fence event 4`, or `cp fence event 0x31`
			when off; `cpfence=mem` writes no event and prints
			neither. Event 4 flushes the timestamp, not the GPU's
			pixel writes, so a boot that reaches the panel on it
			still owes the copy its own CPU-side invalidate.

Two diagnostics ride along and need no boot argument. `cp zap to switch N ms`
prints once, on the boot that publishes the switch: the time between the ZAP
returning and the switch going in, which is the one interval that differs
between a boot that drains it and one that halts on it. `cp notready detail`
follows every `cp NOT ready` line with the rest of `CP_SQE_STAT` (pops 2 and
3), the trust register, the SQE base the CP fetches from, both ring pointers,
`CP_SQE_CNTL`, the fault, interrupt and `RBBM_STATUS3` words, and the CPU's
own copy of ring dwords 8..13: enough to tell a secure-mode hang from a broken
fetch, all plain reads, no new waits. A second `cp notready secure` line reads
`CP_SECURE_MODE`, `CP_APRIV_CNTL`, `CP_CP2GMU_STATUS` and
`RBBM_SECVID_TSB_STATUS_LO`/`_HI`: those four separate "the CP is still in
secure mode" from "the secure TSB is invalid", which the detail line cannot
tell apart.

The submission path prints one line at every step under `gpudump=1`, each
flushed before the next step is entered, so the ring holds the last step that
completed when a submission hands the CPU to a register that never answers:
`uc submit D dwords at 0xN` on entry, `uc keepalive up at 0xN` after the GMU
vote, `uc room wait at 0xN` *before* the first `CP_RB_RPTR` read, `uc room poll
S rptr 0xR room N` on the first eight spins and every 256th after that, `uc room
at 0xN rptr 0xR reads N` once the ring has room, `uc wrap at 0xN`, `uc frame
written at 0xN seq S` once the dwords are in the ring and cleaned, `uc doorbell
0xN` after the `CP_RB_WPTR` store, and `uc submit done seq S ret R` on the way
out. Every wait and every return prints, including the room test's 2 s
patience and both doorbell paths. Reading them: `uc room` repeating with the
same rptr means the CP stopped consuming at the ring, `uc frame written`
followed by silence means the WPTR store or the register access behind it hung,
and `uc submit done` with a `refused` that grows while the room waits mean it is
the 2 s patience that stalled the boot and not the GPU. The 2000-submission
stress prints `stress round K at 0xN refused R` before its first round and every
250 after that, because its 9-dword submission is under the `dwords > 16` gate
on `uc enter`/`uc pre`.

With `gpudump` off the boot differs by three lines only: the first three
submissions print `uc submit D first` before the keepalive write, so a ring that
stops there still says whether the submission path was reached at all.

The wrap publishes the lap end. The tail was always filled with one CP_NOP, but
the write pointer used to jump from the end of a lap to the first submission of
the next one, and the CP resolved the gap by consuming forward: measured with
`gpudump=1`, `uc rptr reg 0x403 sh 0x400 bv 0x0 at 0x3f0 room 18` and the same
poll for the rest of the boot. The read pointer was 19 dwords ahead of the write
cursor, so the 26-room test could only shrink: 2 s a submission, 2000 of them,
and the boot never reached userland. `mondrian_uc_wrap` now cleans the tail,
publishes `CP_RB_WPTR = MONDRIAN_UC_RING_DWORDS` (0x2000: 32 KiB, 8192 dwords)
and waits up to 2000 x 1 ms for `CP_RB_RPTR` to reach the end of the lap. Two
indications are accepted and the line says which: a read pointer at or past the
ring size, or one below the value sampled before the doorbell -- inside a lap
the pointer only moves forward, so a value below it is the CP having passed the
end and restarted. Only then is the cursor rolled to 0, and the cached read
pointer is invalidated so it cannot be used as a bound across the lap it no
longer describes. The room test takes its decision from that cached pointer only
when the room it implies covers the 26 dwords about to be written; a short room
is a fresh `CP_RB_RPTR` read, not the 2 s patience. `cpstress=0` skips the
2000-submission stress entirely: it is a diagnostic, and with anything wrong in
the ring it is 40 minutes of 2 s timeouts before userland starts.

What the next boot's log says:

	MONDRIAN GPU: cp secmode rptr 12 wptr 12
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp ready rptr 25 of 25
	    the CP consumed every bring-up packet; the healthy boot is unchanged
	    apart from the new `cp ready` line.
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp secmode rptr 11 wptr 12
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp secmode retry rptr 15 of 15
	    the race happened and the retry recovered it; the ring is published
	    and submissions fence.
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp NOT ready rptr 11 of 12, sqe 0x649 0x70e60001, ring not published
	    the CP is dead: MondrianGPU does not appear, so Mesa's open fails
	    immediately rather than every frame waiting out a fence.
	MONDRIAN GPU: gpu reset hfi off ack 1
	MONDRIAN GPU: gpu reset hfi on ack 1
	    gpureset=1: the GMU acknowledged both votes, so the CP was re-armed
	    on a core it had just powered off and on.
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp meinit rptr 10 of 10
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp secmode rptr 12 wptr 12
	    cporder=1, or any boot with the default cpapriv=1: ME_INIT was
	    re-offered and the CP retired it before the switch was published;
	    the ready check follows as in any other boot.
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp meinit stuck rptr 0 of 10
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp NOT ready rptr 0 of 10, sqe 0x10e 0x70c80007, ring not published
	    cporder=1 and the CP never fetched the re-offered ME_INIT: nothing
	    else is written, so the ring stays unpublished and MondrianGPU does
	    not appear.
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp apriv 0x4f bv 0xf lpac 0xf lpacprot 0xb
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp meinit rptr 10 of 10
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp secmode rptr 12 wptr 12
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp memwrite rptr 21 wptr 21 canary 0x600dc0de fence 0x600dc0df
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp int 0x0 int0 0x0 ...
	    cpapriv=1: the read-backs prove the CP kept the masks, ME_INIT ran
	    with them, and every privileged packet after it executed -- the
	    event write and the fence land and no illegal-instruction bit is
	    latched. This is the shape of a fixed boot.
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp apriv 0x4f bv 0xf lpac 0xf lpacprot 0xb
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp meinit rptr 10 of 10
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp NOT ready rptr 11 of 12, sqe 0x649 0x70e60001, ring not published
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp notready secure 0x... apriv 0x4f cp2gmu 0x... tsb 0x.../0x...
	    cpapriv=1 and the switch still parks: the privilege side is right,
	    so the secure world is what refuses. `secure` non-zero names the
	    switch itself, the TSB pair names a secure-mode fetch that does not
	    validate, and a cleared apriv says the CP block was reset again
	    behind the ZAP.
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp secmode skipped (cpnosecmode)
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp memwrite rptr 19 wptr 19 canary 0x600dc0de fence 0x0
	    cpnosecmode=1: the CP fetched the first packet after ME_INIT without
	    a switch ever being written, so it was nonsecure already; the memory
	    write executed and the event write behind it did not.
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp protected-mode bracket on
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp memwrite rptr 23 wptr 23 canary 0x600dc0de fence 0x600dc0df
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp ready rptr 31 of 31
	    cpprot=1: the same boot with the bracket; the ring is four dwords
	    longer, so every pointer is four past the unbracketed one, and the
	    event write the boot parks on without it now retires.
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp fence by mem write
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp memwrite rptr 18 wptr 18 canary 0x600dc0de fence 0x600dc0df
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp ready rptr 22 of 22
	    cpfence=mem: both writes to the memptr page retired and their values
	    are the ones the pair asked for; the IB probe follows at 22.
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp fence event 4
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp memwrite rptr 19 wptr 19 canary 0x600dc0de fence 0x600dc0df
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp int 0x0
	    cpevent=4 with the switch skipped: the event write is dispatched
	    where CACHE_CLEAN halted the SQE, so the refusal is the event code
	    and not the opcode. `cp int 0x80` instead says both event types are
	    privileged and the fence has to stop being an event write.
	MONDRIAN GPU: uc frame no protected-mode packet
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp uc stress 2000 refused 0 wait 1 canary 0x... fence 0x...
	    cpapriv=1: the first submission says its frame carries no 0x5f and
	    the stress retires all 2000 of them, so the packet that stopped the
	    CP was CP_SET_PROTECTED_MODE. A stress that still stops behind this
	    line leaves the INDIRECT_BUFFER or the fence inside the submission
	    as the refused packet, and `cpevent=4` / `cpfence=mem` separate those.
	MONDRIAN GPU: uc frame protected-mode bracket
	    cpapriv=0: the frame brackets both halves, as it always did.
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp notready detail sqe 0x... 0x... trust 0x... sqebase ...
	    the CP stopped with the ring published up to the switch; a non-zero
	    trust register or a secure SQE base names the secure handshake, while
	    a clean set of values with rptr still at 11 names the fetch itself.
	MONDRIAN GPU: uc wrap waited rptr 0x... past the lap end
	    gpudump=1, at the end of a lap: the NOP tail is in DRAM, the write
	    pointer named the end of the buffer and the read pointer got there.
	    `wrapped below the lap end` is the same consumption reported by a
	    register masked with the ring size. `uc wrap timeout rptr 0x... seen
	    0x... at 0x...` instead says the CP never took the tail: that
	    submission is refused and the cursor is not rolled, so no dword the
	    CP has not read is overwritten.
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp uc stress 2000 refused 0 wait 1
	    a stress with no `uc no room` and no `uc room poll` spin: the room
	    test reads the register again instead of waiting out its patience.
	MONDRIAN GPU: cp stress skipped
	    cpstress=0: the 2000 submissions are not run; the ring stays the one
	    the bring-up published and the boot goes on.
diff --git a/bsd/dev/mondrian_gpu.c b/bsd/dev/mondrian_gpu.c
index 39feb9b..e59882c 100644
--- a/bsd/dev/mondrian_gpu.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/mondrian_gpu.c
